SECTION 7 — Portworx
================================================================================

PX-01 [B] Portworx node in StorageDown state
  Signal : px_node_status{status="StorageDown"}
           (built-in PX alert: NodeStateChange)
  Playbook: Check underlying block device health on node; check PX journal;
            restart PX service on node; verify multipath

PX-02 [C] PX volume HA level degraded
  Signal : px_volume_ha_level < px_volume_replication_factor for a volume
  Playbook: Check failed replica node; trigger PX volume HA restore once
            node recovers; alert storage admin

PX-03 [C] PX storage pool near capacity
  Signal : px_pool_stats_used_bytes / px_pool_stats_total_bytes > 0.8
  Playbook: Expand pool with new drives; evict/delete old volumes; alert
            storage capacity team

PX-04 [C] PX volume I/O latency high
  Signal : px_volume_read_latency_seconds or px_volume_write_latency_seconds
           p99 > threshold
  Playbook: Check node disk I/O saturation; consider volume migration to
            faster pool; check network for shared NVMe

PX-05 [B] PX cluster quorum lost
  Signal : px_cluster_status{status="NotInQuorum"} or PX alert
           NodeDecommissionedInvalid via Alertmanager webhook
  Playbook: Page on-call immediately; restore failed PX nodes; do not attempt
            volume operations until quorum restored

PX-06 [B] PX internal kvdb failure
  Signal : px_kvdb_health_state != 1; also PX alert KVDBNotSetup
  Playbook: Check PX kvdb pods; if external etcd used check etcd health;
            restart PX on affected nodes in sequence

PX-07 [E] PX volume stuck detaching
  Signal : K8s VolumeAttachment stuck Attached when expected Detached via
           Splunk; PX volume state=detaching > 5 minutes
  Playbook: Force-detach via pxctl; confirm consumer pod is fully terminated;
            check PX journal for I/O errors

